\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico judge's order takes heat off Zedillo. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Stott \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, March 6 (Reuter) - A judge on Monday ordered the brother of Mexico's former president to stand trial on murder charges, taking some pressure off embattled President Ernesto Zedillo as his government sought to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sinking to new lows and Indian rebels from revolting. \par \par \uc2  Judge Diogenes Cruz ruled there was enough evidence to try Raul Salinas de Gortari, the 48-year-old elder brother of ex-president Carlos Salinas, on charges of masterminding the murder last year of the boss of the ruling Institutional Revolutionary Party (PRI). \par \par \uc2  The arrest rocked Mexico where ex-presidents and their families have for generations enjoyed de facto immunity from the law after leaving office. \par \par \uc2  In a separate development, prosecutors said they were seeking the extradition from the United States to Mexico of Mario Ruiz Massieu, brother of the dead party leader, on charges of covering up the murder investigation to favour Raul Salinas. \par \par \uc2  Ruiz Massieu was arrested at the Newark, New Jersey, airport in the northeastern U.S. last Friday for failing to declare to customs officials all of the $46,000 in cash he was carrying in a suitcase on an unexplained flight to Spain. Under U.S. law all people entering or leaving the country must declare \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in excess of $10,000. \par \par \uc2  A U.S. judge on Monday ordered Ruiz Massieu held without bail after Mexico demanded his extradition and his lawyer said his client was seeking political asylum in the United States. \par \par \uc2  Both Ruiz Matthieu and Raul Salinas deny any wrongdoing and say the charges against them are absurd and unproven. \par \par \uc2  The moves against Salinas and Ruiz Massieu were likely to help Zedillo who is facing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis and a deepening economic recession by showing he was serious about promises to bring high-level wrongdoers to trial, political analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Zedillo's image took a dent over the weekend when he sent an envoy to negotiate a truce with Carlos Salinas who was so upset by unproven allegations that he too was linked to the September 1994 murder of PRI boss Francisco Ruiz Massieu that he started a hunger strike. \par \par \uc2  The truce which involved a public statement from prosecutors making clear Carlos Salinas was not under suspicion for the assassination halted the hunger strike but did Zedillo little good with public opinion or with opposition politicians. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's financial markets kept up the pressure on Zedillo's administration Monday where the peso plunged to a new record low of 6.85 to the dollar on fears the economic crisis would trigger a series of bank collapses. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm traders and by late afternoonn peso futures had limped back to a buy-sell rate of 6.53/58, down 25 centavos from their close last Friday. \par \par \uc2  On the political front, Indian rebels in the southern state of Chiapas dealt Zedillo a blow on Sunday night by rejecting his proposals for peace and dialogue in the 14-month conflict as "a real backward step" though they stressed they were still interested in talking rather than fighting. \par \par \uc2  In another reminder of the smouldering tensions in Chiapas, ranchers and right-wingers burnt the symbolic coffins of two pro-dialogue figures in a public square at the weekend to show their opposition to negotiations with the rebels of the Zapatista National Liberation Army. \par \par \uc2  Back in the capital, Zedillo's government faced another test in Congress where politicians this week were due to start picking over the controversial $20 billion U.S. aid package to Mexico agreed by Washington with Zedillo's government at the end of last month. \par \par \uc2  The deal is likely to go through although some Mexicans are unhappy about a clause allowing the Americans first call on their precious oil revenues in the event of a default. Legislators are also concerned the severe austerity measures imposed by the accord would kill economic growth. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr3600jm1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
