\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's New Peso -2: Down 39% Since Crisis Erupted \b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 1995\par 22:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Traders said the new peso may slowly be finding its natural level following weeks of volatility and flirtations with the psycholical barrier of 6 pesos to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  'It seems every time we approach six, the market brings the rate down again,' said one Mexico City trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders said, though, that the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached the level of 6 pesos to the dollar in intraday trading. They said there was no indication of new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Miguel Mancera said Wednesday he expected the peso to shortly stabilize around 5 new pesos to the dollar. Mexican officials had earlier maintained the peso would stabilize at 4.50 new pesos to the dollar, but Finance Minister Guillermo Ortiz also said Wednesday the government would have to re-examine its targets. \par \par \uc2  The new peso strengthened 2.7% against the dollar from Wednesday's close, but still stands 39% down against the dollar since Dec. 20, when Mexico's financial crisis erupted. \par \par \uc2  Mexico was forced to move to a free floating exchange regime when its international reserves dipped to such low levels it could no longer sustain a long-held policy of defending the new peso within a wide trading band with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 23-02-95 \par \par \uc2  2114GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr2n01a6a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscal