\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico stocks fall to 17-month low, peso weakens. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Martin Langfield \par \uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (Reuter) - The Mexican peso plunged and the stock market fell to a 17-month low Wednesday as foreign investors fled the nation, worried about the economy and the indigenous uprising in the southern state of Chiapas. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso fell sharply, ending at about 5.97 per dollar after dipping below 6 to the dollar during the day. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the peso, selling about $100 million to buy the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The peso opened the day under heavy pressure from foreign investors, but when it hit 6 (pesos to the dollar), the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The new peso has shed 42 percent of its value since the government decided on Dec. 20 to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then let it float on world markets. \par \par \uc2  Traders said foreign investors were troubled by the political situation in Chiapas and confused about President Ernesto Zedillo's decision on Tuesday to halt the army's advance on Zapatista rebels there, they added. \par \par \uc2  Stock prices took a tumble for the first time since the announcement two weeks ago of a $50 billion U.S.-led aid package for the Mexican economy. \par \par \uc2  The main stock market index closed at a 17-month low of 1,798.0, down 123.08 points or 6.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Rumours, later denied, that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Miguel Mancera would resign hit stock prices. \par \par \uc2  They were then compounded by a statement by Grupo Sidek, a Mexican conglomerate with vast interests in steel and tourism, that it would default on some short-term debt contracted by its subsidiaries -- the first default announcement in the two-month old economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  The share price of Sidek and its subsidiaries Simex and Situr fell more than 20 percent on the news amid concerns that other companies might also face liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  "This is the tip of the iceberg," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  "The market was sad and bad. It is still reflecting what is happening in the country," one trader said. "There is a complete and utter lack of confidence among investors, and the rumours add to that." \par \par \uc2  Stock prices were also hit by a rise in domestic interest rates in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly auction of government securities. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 28-day Mexican Treasury bill rate rose 4.8 percentage points to 40 percent in the auction as Banco de Mexico cranked up rates to try and entice investors. \par \par \uc2  Investors' appetite for the bills was weak, with demand for all T-bills falling to 3.45 billion new pesos, less than half of the 7.49 billion new pesos investors sought last week. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr2g0138f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
