\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico stocks, peso reel as foreigners bail out. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Martin Langfield \par \uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Feb 15 (Reuter) - The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a battering Wednesday and stock prices fell sharply as foreign investors pulled out of their Mexican holdings and bought dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The latest round of market turmoil came after a cocktail of news about the financial health of Mexican companies and the indigenous uprising in the southern state of Chiapas that disturbed investors even as the government was moving to reestablish stability on the political and economic fronts. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican new peso -- so called after the government slashed three zeroes off the old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1993 -- fell sharply, ending at about 5.97 per dollar after at one point dipping below 6 to the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the peso, selling about $100 million to buy the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The peso opened the day under heavy pressure from foreign investors, but when it hit 6 (new pesos to the dollar), the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said foreign investor uncertainty over the political situation in the troubled state of Chiapas caused some of the pressure. Foreign investors do not know how to interpret President Ernesto Zedillo's decision Tuesday to halt the army's advance on Zapatista rebels there, they added. \par \par \uc2  Stock prices also took a tumble for the first time since the announcement two weeks ago of a $50 billion U.S.-led aid package for the Mexican economy. \par \par \uc2  The main stock market index was down more than 100 points, or over 5 percent, in late trading. \par \par \uc2  Rumours, later denied, that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Miguel Mancera would resign hit stock prices. \par \par \uc2  They were compounded by steel firm Sidek's statement that it would default on some short-term debt contracted by its subsidiaries -- the first default announcement of the two-month old economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  The share price of Sidek and its subsidiaries Simex and Situr all fell more than 25 percent on the news amid concerns that other companies might also face liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of confusion in the market and the foreigners are selling most," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  A Banco de Mxico spokesman said rumours of Mancera's resignation were "totally unfounded". \par \par \uc2  Stock prices were also hit by a rise in domestic interest rates in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly auction of government securities. The benchark 28-day Mexican Treasury bill rate rose 4.8 percentage points to 40 percent in the auction as Banco de Mexico cranked up rates to try and entice investors. \par \par \uc2  Investors' appetite for the bills was weak, with demand for all T-bills falling to 3.45 billion new pesos, less than half of the 7.49 billion new pesos investors sought last week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that in the securities market, too, confusion was prevalent about Zedillo's aims in Chiapas after he first sent police and troops to detain rebel leader Marcos and then ordered security forces to halt their advance. \par \par \uc2  "Because there was such a drastic change of signal in the handling of the problem, investors sought higher returns," one analyst said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr2f015uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic