\par\par \b \uc2  Mexico's New Peso Closes Down At 5.7450 Pesos Per Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 1995\par 21:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY (AP-DJ)--Mexico's new peso closed down but slightly higher than intraday lows Tuesday after alleged late \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  propped up the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City, the spot and 24-hour dollar ended up at middle rate levels of 5.7450 pesos from 5.7150 pesos at Monday's close. The intraday high for the dollar was 5.8250 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened on the continued political scramble in the U.S. to approve 40 billion dlrs in bond guarantees for Mexico and disappointing demand at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly auction for dollar-linked tesobonos. \par \par \uc2  But traders said the late return of the peso was directly linked to alleged Bank of Mexico buying of 500 million dlrs of pesos through an unknown number of private banks. That figure could not be confirmed and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials were not immediately available for comment. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 24-01-95 \par \par \uc2  2000GMT \par \par \uc2  1027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr1o00brq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
