\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. helps prop up peso as Mexico stocks plunge. \b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY (Reuter) - The U.S. Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday to prop up the battered Mexican peso, but while the move helped the peso, Mexican stocks plummeted in the biggest one-day drop in five years. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Mexican officials were seeking support abroad to help the country weather its financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The flotation of the peso on December 20, which resulted in its losing nearly 40 per cent of its value, led investors to bail out of Mexican investments, sending the stock market skidding. \par \par \uc2  In New York, a spokesman for the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, which generally acts for the Federal Reserve (Fed) in market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said it bought Mexican pesos for dollars at the request of the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  These \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are consistent with the efforts of the U.S. monetary authorities to assist Mexico in responding to recent financial developments,'' the Fed, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is known, said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Banco de Mexico, said it had made the first drawings on a U.S.-Canada peso support package announced last week, using $500 million from U.S. monetary authorities and C$83 million ($59 million U.S.) from Canada. \par \par \uc2  The peso regained some of its massive losses of the last three weeks, rising as high as 5.0 to the dollar before easing to end with a gain of 30 centavos at 5.30. \par \par \uc2  But the moves to support the peso could not stem the losses in Mexican stocks. \par \par \uc2  The bolsa's IPC index of 37 shares closed down 149.88 points, or 6.65 percent, at 2,104.05. In percentage terms, it was the steepest stock market drop since October 16, 1989, when the bolsa fell 8.05 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr1a004ay\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
