\par\par \b \uc2  Fed moves to support peso as Mexicans seek support. \b0\par\par\uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY (Reuter) - The U.S. Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday to prop up the battered Mexican peso, but while the move helped the peso, Mexican stocks plummeted in the biggest one-day drop in five years. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Mexican officials were seeking support abroad to help the country weather its financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The flotation of the peso on Dec. 20, which resulted in its losing nearly 40 percent of its value, led investors to bail out of Mexican investments, sending the stock market skidding. \par \par \uc2  In New York, a spokesman for the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, which generally acts for the Fed in market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said it bought Mexican pesos for dollars at the request of the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "These \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are consistent with the efforts of the U.S. monetary authorities to assist Mexico in responding to recent financial developments," the Fed said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Banco de Mexico, said it had made the first drawings on a U.S.-Canada peso support package announced last week, using $500 million from U.S. monetary authorities and C$83 million ($59 million) from Canada. \par \par \uc2  The peso regained some of its massive losses of the last three weeks, rising as high as 5.0 to the dollar before easing to end with a gain of 30 centavos at 5.30. \par \par \uc2  But the moves to support the peso could not stem the losses in Mexican stocks. \par \par \uc2  The bolsa's IPC index of 37 shares closed down 149.88 points, or 6.65 percent, at 2,104.05. In percentage terms, it was the steepest stock market drop since Oct. 16, 1989, when the bolsa fell 8.05 percent. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, Mexican Foreign Minister Jose Angel Gurria, putting a brave face on his country's peso crisis to placate nervous Japanese investors, forecast an end to the upheaval in a few weeks. \par \par \uc2  In meetings with government officials, bankers and business executives in Tokyo, Gurria reiterated Mexico's position that its economy is fundamentally strong and said the crisis is an opportunity, not a setback. \par \par \uc2  "In the next few weeks I expect the pendulum to have swung back to the middle," Gurria said. \par \par \uc2  He forecast the peso would settle around 4.50 to the dollar but gave no timing for such stability to be achieved. \par \par \uc2  Governor Hiroshi Yasuda of The Export-Import Bank of Japan praised the rescue effort Mexico has put in place involving deregulation, monetary controls and privatisation. \par \par \uc2  "The new programme has good substance," he said. "But we want to watch how the new measures take effect for a while. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico, a Finance Ministry official said annualized inflation will soar to 31.1 percent and real growth in the gross domestic product will drop to 1.1. percent in the first three months of 1995. \par \par \uc2  The official, Martin Werner, unveiled figures projecting annualized growth of just 0.2 percent and annualized inflation of 21.2 percent in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  But he said the economy will then recover, allowing for higher growth in 1996. \par \par \uc2  "The problem of the adjustment is transitory and the economy is going to come out of it with more solid bases to re-establish the path of growth," Werner told a meeting of bankers and investors at the Mexican stock market. \par \par \uc2  One key political opposition party has filed a formal complaint charging former President Carlos Salinas de Gortari covered up Mexico's economic problems to help his bid to become the head of the new World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  The complaint against Salinas, given to the attorney general's office for investigation by the centre-left Democratic Revolution Party, or PRD, blamed the Salinas administration for the country's current crisis, which began with the peso devaluation. \par \par \uc2  It accused Salinas of "crimes against the national wealth" and "improper exercise of public service." \par \par \uc2  In its complaint, the PRD said Salinas and his economic cabinet allowed the country's current account deficit to grow to "an unsustainable level" and failed to take action for fear it would taint Salinas' reputation as the man who brought economic stability to Mexico. \par \par \uc2  It said the peso should have been devalued last April to put a damper on the deficit, which grew to $28 billion by the year's end. \par \par \uc2  Salinas, whose six-year term ended Dec. 1, is on a world tour seeking support for the WTO post and has refused to comment on the accusations. \par \par \uc2  The attorney general's office accepted the PRD complaint but gave no indication of what action it would take. \par \par \uc2  Attorney general Antonio Lozano, a member of the conservative National Action Party, was appointed by Salinas' successor Ernesto Zedillo. Lozano is the first cabinet member in modern Mexican history who is not a member of Zedillo's Institutional Revolutionary Party. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr1a004aq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
