\par\par \b \uc2  (WSJ):U.S. Fed  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  To Bolster  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's Peso \b0\par\par\uc2  By TIM CARRINGTON \par \uc2 330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 1995\par 08:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON (AP-DJ)--The Federal Reserve, broadening efforts to restore investor confidence in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the reeling peso, Tuesday's Wall Street Journal reports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was viewed as comparatively light. But it was enough to shore up the sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to send a critical signal that the U.S. wasn't willing to stand by while the peso continued to slide. \par \par \uc2  The peso ended the day up 5.75% against the dollar, at 5.39 up from Friday's close of 5.70 pesos. \par \par \uc2  But it was unclear whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have any lasting impact on the peso, which has lost more than one-third of its value since Dec. 20. In a sign that investor confidence remains shaky, Mexican stocks Monday suffered their largest one-day loss in a year, with the key IPC index off almost 7%, dropping 149.88 points to 2,104.05. \par \par \uc2  International investors, while not entirely surprised by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , applauded the Fed's move. '\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's too important to the U.S.,' said Joyce Cornell, a portfolio manager specializing in emerging markets at Scudder, Stevens & Clark, Inc. 'We're too intertwined to let it deteriorate.' \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was made at the request of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Banco de Mexico, which financed the move by tapping an 18-billion-dlr emergency credit facility that was set up by a U.S.-led international group after Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  began its free-fall last month. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrew 500 million dlrs from a 9-billion-dlr U.S. credit line and 83 million CAN dlrs from a 1.5-billion-CAN-dlr credit facility. The Federal Reserve then apparently used some of the 559 million dollars to buy pesos and sell dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 10-01-95 \par \par \uc2  0700GMT \par \par \uc2  1127 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr1a002pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
