\par\par \b \uc2  Fed moves to support peso as Mexicans seek support. \b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Reuter) - The Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday to prop up the battered Mexican peso as Mexican officials sought support abroad to help the country weather its financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The flotation of the peso on Dec. 20, which resulted in its losing about a third of its value, led investors to bail out of Mexican investments, sending the Mexican stock market skidding. \par \par \uc2  A spokesman for the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, which generally acts for the Fed in market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said it bought Mexican pesos for dollars at the request of the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In a statement, the Fed said it "announced today that it is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the request of and for the account of the Banco de Mexico, purchasing pesos against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "These \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are consistent with the efforts of the U.S. monetary authorities to assist Mexico in responding to recent financial developments." \par \par \uc2  The peso regained some of its massive losses of the last three weeks, rising as much as 65 centavos to 5.0 to the dollar before easing to 5.30. \par \par \uc2  But Mexican stocks slumped more than 74 points, or 3.28 percent, showing no sign of a turnaround. \par \par \uc2  Mexican Foreign Minister Jose Angel Gurria, putting a brave face on his country's peso crisis to placate nervous Japanese investors, forecast an end to the upheaval in a few weeks. \par \par \uc2  In meetings with government officials, bankers and business executives in Tokyo, Gurria reiterated Mexico's position that its economy is fundamentally strong and said the crisis is an opportunity, not a setback. \par \par \uc2  "In the next few weeks I expect the pendulum to have swung back to the middle," Gurria said. \par \par \uc2  He forecast that the peso would settle around 4.50 to the dollar but gave no timing for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability be achieved. \par \par \uc2  Gurria received a polite hearing. But like much of the financial world, his listeners were not ready to buy his argument completely that a drastic shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value was something to cheer about. \par \par \uc2  Japan has agreed to take part in a U.S.-led $18 billion credit arrangement to see Mexico through the immediate crisis. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Japan was expected to contribute up to $1 billion, while four private Japanese banks are part of other rescue efforts led by U.S. commercial banks. \par \par \uc2  But bankers and business officials were cautious. \par \par \uc2  Governor Hiroshi Yasuda of The Export-Import Bank of Japan praised the rescue effort Mexico has put in place involving deregulation, monetary controls and privatisation. \par \par \uc2  "The new programme has good substance," he said. "But we want to watch how the new measures take effect for a while. \par \par \uc2  "Having just joined NAFTA, they have a tough road ahead but they have to hang in there," he said, referring to the North American Free Trade Agreement signed by Mexico, the United States and Canada. \par \par \uc2  Asked about fresh lending to Mexico, Yasuda said, "It would be no problem if the Mexican economy heads for better conditions after global financial aid takes effect." \par \par \uc2  At another meeting in London, investors and analysts were told by a high-level Mexican delegation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso, despite the statement to the contrary by the New York Fed. \par \par \uc2  "They said the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not supporting the peso," said one analyst who attended the meeting. \par \par \uc2  "They said they would not be active but were confident the market would take the peso to 4.5 per dollar," another said. \par \par \uc2  Francisco Gil, vice-governor of Bank of Mexico, Enrique Vilatela, director of Banco Mexicano de Comercio Exterior (Bancomex), Carlos Sales, economic aide to President Ernesto Zedillo, and Rolf Schlettwein, vice-chairman of Mexican Council of Investment, were at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011104dr190049s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
